  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nA warm & dry day is ongoing, ahead of a Canadian cold front which\nis moving south of Calgary, where it is currently 52F and \nraining. We will see temps well into the 80s to near 90F today. \nTemps will be slow to cool along the foothills with lee side \npressure falls this evening, and in fact there should be briefly \nbreezy downslope winds (gusts 30 mph?) at places like Red Lodge, \nFort Smith & Sheridan between 5-10z. It appears there will be a \npre-frontal wind shift to N-NE this evening, then main cold \nadvection arrives late tonight into early Friday. Tomorrow will be\nnotably cooler than today with highs in the lower 60s north to \nmid 70s south.\n\nSome light showers are expected tomorrow into tomorrow night per a\nweak shortwave on top of the cooler surface with developing\nupslope/easterly winds. As a moister surface high settles in,\nthere is a good chance of low stratus/fog particularly in upslope\nareas Friday night into Saturday. This will be something to \nmonitor but high res models are starting to point in that \ndirection, which conceptually is to be expected. Low amplitude \nridging develops Saturday but cooler surface high will remain on \nthe east side of the mountains. With expectation of some low \nclouds and upslope/easterly winds, have cooled forecast highs to a\nfew degrees below the typical guidance. Elevated instability \nalong a mid level warm front may support a few showers/t-storms in\nfar southeast MT Saturday night, otherwise there should be a \nperiod of dry wx associated with the brief ridging.\n\nStronger Pacific low moves thru the PacNW Saturday night and \nbegins to provide some large scale ascent to our west on Sunday. \nHowever, the track of the low from eastern OR thru northwest MT \ndoes not favor us for precip, but rather dry slotting. This is the\nprimary reason for the forecast trending drier. Models are showing\nlee side pressure falls as this happens Sunday, which would not \nonly introduce drier downslope winds but also warmer temps. After\ncooler days Friday/Saturday it looks as if Sunday will be warmer \nespecially in central and southern parts of our cwa where highs \ncould push 80F. Greatest potential for precip seems to be in the \neast Sunday night, with increasing elevated instability combined \nwith the ascent suggesting scattered showers and thunderstorms, \nand cannot rule out a few strong storms depending on the evolution\nof a surface trof. Western mountains and the north (away from \ndownslope impacts) should also see some showers as the trof \npasses. Southern foothills like Red Lodge and Sheridan will be \nmostly (if not entirely) dry over these next few days. Mountain\nsnow potential has cratered. Look for scattered snow showers with\nlittle to no accumulation above 10kft Sunday into Sunday night.\n\nMonday will be post-frontal and cooler w/ breezy/drier W-NW winds.\nGusts may approach 40 mph over the east. A secondary wave dropping\nout of the NW may or may not be strong enough for some showers\ninto Tuesday but it will help to keep temps cooler than normal\nTuesday and Wednesday with highs in the 60s to lower 70s. \nNighttime lows will be seasonably chilly but the risk of a frost \nis under 50%. In fact, frontal winds will keep temps somewhat \nelevated Sunday night. Monday night and Tuesday night should be \nthe coolest nights of next week (lows mid 30s to mid 40s).\n\nOverwhelming model consensus is for building heights, dry and\nwarmer weather for the latter part of next week. Look for highs in\nthe 70s (near normal) Thursday, then likely 80+ degrees by the end\nof the week.\n\nJKL",
